INTRO=[
"Satellite gravimetry can identify broad water-storage change, but identifying its causes requires additional assumptions about storage compartments, natural variability and human intervention. A groundwater anomaly obtained by subtracting non-groundwater storage from GRACE is already a combined observational–model estimate. Its trend can therefore depend on the processed gravity product, the land-surface representation and the spatial support of the subtraction. A statistically significant local decline is not, by itself, a measured pumping signal.",
"Thailand illustrates the resulting inference problem. Earlier studies have examined storage dynamics and extremes in the Chao Phraya system and have compared derived groundwater storage with in situ observations (Abhishek et al., 2021; Sharma et al., 2025). Water-budget research also identifies the value of multisource information in this setting (Abhishek et al., 2022). These contributions establish that storage separation and groundwater comparison are not new to Thailand. The outstanding question addressed here is how robust the attribution remains when the processed product, climatic information and monitored well population are examined together across a national basin framework.",
"A further ambiguity arises when machine learning is trained directly on observed storage and its prediction is named a climate-only baseline. The observed target can already contain management effects, and some candidate predictors, including actual evapotranspiration and soil moisture, can respond to irrigation. A fitted relationship may be useful for reconstruction without identifying an unmodified climatic counterfactual. A documented naturalized hydrological experiment provides a more explicit baseline, although its regional accuracy must still be evaluated (Müller Schmied et al., 2024).",
"Groundwater observations do not automatically resolve that ambiguity. Wells measure local head rather than regional water mass. Available records can mix aquifers and aggregation intervals, and the set of monitored stations can change. A positive correlation with a total-storage residual may reflect groundwater behaviour, shared climatic forcing, a common trend or network composition. A strong association for one chosen panel is therefore less informative than a comparison that preserves common time support and tests how the observation network affects it.",
"We develop an attribution-credibility assessment combining three linked experiments. First, documented CSR RL06.3 storage is compared with a contrasting COST-G/G3P chain on common support, while native mascon mapping exposes shared basin representation. Second, naturalized-model reconstruction is tested through controlled climate-memory ablations and frozen-model precipitation replacement. Third, the paired hydrological residual is assessed against observed heads using station resampling and common-period balanced panels. The accounting identity and learning algorithms are established tools; the contribution is the controlled integration and the empirical evidence it reveals.",
"The analysis addresses all 22 principal basins and national context. It asks whether broad decline and basin ordering are equally robust, whether climate memory adds information beyond seasonality, and whether groundwater agreement supports an interpretation of the residual as human depletion. The expected output is an evidence-qualified assessment rather than a predetermined human fraction. Reproducible tables and an interactive delivery system make both supporting and limiting evidence available for review."
]
METHODS=[
('2.1 Study domain and documented inputs',[
"We use the supplied Office of the National Water Resources principal-basin geometry and polygon–grid intersection weights. Separately coded island features are grouped with their parent basin. The 22-basin framework distinguishes the principal basin labelled Chao Phraya from upstream basins such as Ping, Wang, Yom and Nan; it is therefore not identical to every greater-Chao-Phraya domain in earlier literature. National intersection-area support is approximately 515,230 km².",
"The primary gravity input is the CSR RL06.3 all-corrections record, downloaded with the provider land mask and native-mascon mapping. Source checksums and a nominal-month audit are retained. Units are converted from centimetres to millimetres; source time attributes are decoded explicitly. Special solutions centred in late October 2011 and April 2015 are assigned to nominal November 2011 and May 2015, respectively, following the established special-month convention. No mission-gap interpolation, additional empirical smoothing or gain factor is applied (Save et al., 2016; CSR, 2026; Sutterley et al., 2026).",
"The principal TWSA period is April 2002–September 2024, containing 237 observed solutions. Surface-water coverage limits the full-balance GWSA record to September 2023 and 225 observed months. COST-G/G3P v1.12 and the original unidentified export are sensitivity records. Their satellite information is not independent of CSR, and the unidentified export is not treated as an additional identified solution. GLDAS Noah supplies soil, canopy and snow storage; G3P supplies surface water and related structural alternatives (Rodell et al., 2004; Güntner et al., 2024).",
"WaterGAP 2.2e naturalized and historical-human runs share GSWP3–ERA5 forcing and extend through 2023. Ten regional variable/scenario subsets are retained. Monthly identities from their synthetic 360-day calendar are mapped to month-start labels without daily interpolation. Meteorological rates are expressed as mean millimetres per day rather than converted to physical monthly totals using an assumed synthetic-month length. The supporting 2003–2024 cube contains CHIRPS precipitation, GLEAM and MODIS ET, surface and root-zone moisture and land-surface temperature (Funk et al., 2015; Martens et al., 2017; Gruber et al., 2019)."
]),
('2.2 Storage balance and spatial-support controls',[
"Basin means use polygon–grid intersection areas and the fraction of valid basin area for each month. A minimum 50 percent area threshold is required. Coarse component and model cells are assigned to aligned quarter-degree locations using nearest-parent replication, which creates no new independent spatial information. The groundwater calculation uses a common valid-cell mask and a common 2004–2009 observed reference for every compartment.",
"The full balance subtracts soil-moisture, surface-water, canopy and snow anomalies from the matching TWSA reference. Both grid-level and regional identities are verified. A filtered root-zone soil alternative changes the soil representation while retaining the CSR baseline. The native G3P groundwater product provides a full-chain comparison; because gravity processing and compartment definitions differ, its disagreement cannot be assigned to a single source of error. Compartment compatibility remains structural uncertainty rather than a corrected quantity (Sharifi et al., 2025).",
"Processed products are compared on identical valid cells and months and rebased to a common valid reference. Seasonal Sen slopes and their basin ranks are compared separately. Native weights are aggregated by CSR mascon identifier; inverse weight concentration and pairwise shared weight describe mapped spatial support. These diagnostics are not counts of independent information or estimates of gravity-error covariance. We follow the provider's restriction to basin time-series interpretation and retain a small-basin caution for every principal basin (Longuevergne et al., 2010; CSR, 2026)."
]),
('2.3 Trend estimation and conditional inference',[
"Seasonal Sen slopes are the median of same-calendar-month pairwise rates using actual elapsed years. Original and seasonal Mann–Kendall tests are retained, with Benjamini–Hochberg adjustment within each 22-basin storage family. Missing dates are not compressed or filled. Primary slope intervals use 300 calendar-year residual-block resamples and describe uncertainty within the selected processed record rather than complete product uncertainty (Sen, 1968; Hirsch et al., 1982; Hirsch and Slack, 1984; Benjamini and Hochberg, 1995).",
"Fixed early, later, pre-gap and post-gap windows assess period dependence. Short-window slopes are descriptive, not acceleration estimates or forecasts. Cross-product Spearman coefficients describe rank stability; their p-values are not interpreted as independent spatial replication because basin information overlaps. Direction agreement and magnitude differences are reported alongside ranking.",
"For additive attribution we fit OLS with a time trend and calendar-month fixed effects on identical observed dates for all components. A shared-sign calendar-block wild regression bootstrap produces 999 conditional slope replicates for block lengths of one, two and three years. Original missingness and the component identities are retained. The resulting percentile intervals are unstudentized conditional assessments; they do not propagate all gravity, forcing and structural uncertainties or provide a complete small-sample inferential guarantee (Cameron et al., 2008)."
]),
('2.4 Climate memory and independent forcing stress tests',[
"The principal climate emulators reconstruct naturalized WaterGAP storage from precipitation, potential evapotranspiration, causal weather lags, exponentially weighted memories and seasonal harmonics. Warm-up uses 1981–1990, training 1991–2009, validation 2010–2015 and testing 2016–2023. Tuning and transformations use chronological training folds. Ridge, ElasticNet, splines and Random Forest form the original candidate ensemble; validation fixes member selection and weights before testing (Hoerl and Kennard, 1970; Breiman, 2001; Zou and Hastie, 2005; Roberts et al., 2017).",
"Controlled Ridge ablations compare five prespecified feature groups: season only, current weather with seasonality, lagged weather, lagged weather plus memory, and weather memory without seasonal harmonics. Precipitation and potential-ET lags are 0, 1, 2, 3, 6 and 12 months; memory scales are 1, 3, 6, 12 and 24 months. Test metrics include raw monthly scores and anomaly NSE after subtracting a training-derived climatology. They verify a parent model, not independent storage observations (Gupta et al., 2009; Knoben et al., 2019).",
"A frozen full Ridge model is then driven with unadjusted CHIRPS precipitation and with a training-only calendar-month mean adjustment. CHIRPS totals are divided by actual calendar-month days to match the mean-rate convention. The target remains the native-forced naturalized simulation, so this is a covariate-shift experiment rather than a ranking of rainfall accuracy. Memory error reduction is assessed with 999 circular paired two-year resamples of the eight test years.",
"A complementary observed-response test selects precipitation accumulations of 1, 3, 6 or 12 months and lags of 0–6 months in 2003–2015. Development-only seasonal and linear-trend references are retained when evaluating 2016–2023. The selected relationship is frozen before the later-period correlation is calculated. Whole-year precipitation permutations and basin FDR provide a conditional screen. These empirical delays are not interpreted as unique recharge or aquifer residence times."
]),
('2.5 Paired model attribution and discrepancy',[
"Let O denote observed storage, C the naturalized model and A the paired historical-human model. We calculate the modeled direct-human perturbation H=A−C, the observed residual R=O−C and discrepancy E=O−A. Every term shares the valid observed footprint and common reference. The monthly and fitted-trend identities are checked exactly. Sen component slopes remain separate because medians are not additive.",
"The descriptive magnitude diagnostic D=|βE|/(|βH|+|βE|) measures whether the discrepancy trend is large relative to the represented human perturbation. It is not a causal human fraction, a proportion of observed decline or an allocation of independent error variances. Shared bootstrap multipliers provide conditional intervals while preserving the trend identities.",
"H is a change in the human perturbation represented by the model relative to the anomaly reference; it is not cumulative depletion before GRACE. E may include unrepresented human intervention as well as model, forcing, observation and compartment errors. Neither the full residual nor the full discrepancy is assigned a unique physical cause. This makes the conditions for a human-depletion interpretation explicit instead of hiding them in a residual label."
]),
('2.6 Groundwater evidence and network uncertainty',[
"The GROW extraction retains 5,484 actual aggregates from 511 Thai wells: 5,259 annual aggregates from 506 wells and 225 monthly aggregates from five wells. Provider flags and aggregation intervals are preserved; gap-filled groundwater values are excluded. Signed head is negative depth below ground. Descriptive well-standardized indices are combined by coordinate-station median before equal-weight regional averaging. No specific yield or storativity is invented, because aquifer construction and storage coefficients are insufficiently documented (Bäthge et al., 2026).",
"Annual head comparisons use annual storage means with at least eight observed months and exclude incomplete endpoint years. Raw agreement is supplemented by climate-controlled, detrended and consecutive-observed-year-change associations; nonconsecutive years are not treated as annual differences. A 499-replicate coordinate-station bootstrap resamples complete station trajectories and quantifies network sensitivity conditional on the observed years. It is not a temporal or causal correlation interval.",
"Five conditions are retained: longest overlap with flags, exclusion of flagged observations, a wider-history panel eligible under an eight-of-ten-year rule, all available wells restricted to 2013–2022, and a strict ten-of-ten-year panel on that same window. The last two conditions were added as an explicitly exploratory network extension. Their common observed storage years distinguish a time-window change from a panel-composition change. A separate well-trend screen requires at least eight annual observations and an eight-year span, then groups colocated trajectories for direction summaries."
])]
RESULTS=[
('3.1 Broad decline persists but basin ranking is product dependent',[
"National CSR TWSA declines at −7.51 mm/year over the full record, with a conditional 95 percent interval of −9.79 to −5.68. Primary GWSA declines at −6.84 mm/year, with an interval of −8.77 to −5.11. All 22 principal basins have negative primary slope estimates, although their magnitudes differ greatly and all remain below the provider's approximate small-basin caution area.",
"On the common 223-month source support, national TWSA slopes are −7.67 mm/year for CSR and −2.56 mm/year for COST-G/G3P; corresponding groundwater slopes are −6.88 and −3.45 mm/year. Basin rank agreement is weak, with Spearman coefficients 0.138 and 0.027. The legacy export closely resembles CSR numerically but cannot be counted as independent identified replication. The national sign is therefore more stable than a local severity ordering.",
"The filtered-soil CSR groundwater alternative declines at −7.86 mm/year nationally, retaining the sign but changing the magnitude by about 1.02 mm/year. This and the full-chain comparison demonstrate distinct structural sensitivities. Native mapping identifies 68 touched mascons nationally and an inverse weight-concentration count near 50.1, while individual basins have smaller, uneven support. These are representation diagnostics rather than independent sample sizes."
]),
('3.2 Climate memory adds transferable information within the parent model',[
"Current-weather Ridge models give national held-out anomaly NSE of 0.283 for TWSA and 0.039 for GWSA. Adding lagged weather and memory raises those values to 0.890 and 0.723. Basin-median anomaly NSE rises from approximately 0.201 to 0.844 for TWSA and from −0.044 to 0.680 for GWSA. Seasonal-only models remain weak for anomalies, confirming that strong recurring seasonality is not the main explanation for the memory-model improvement.",
"The previously selected multi-family ensembles also reconstruct the native parent target strongly, with national raw monthly NSE near 0.981 and 0.961. These scores have a different evaluation target from the CSR model–observation comparison. They do not establish that the naturalized simulation or its groundwater trend is correct. The matched land-compartment GLDAS comparison remains a cross-model benchmark rather than a groundwater observation.",
"Frozen-model replacement with unadjusted CHIRPS reduces national anomaly NSE to 0.688 for TWSA and 0.562 for GWSA. Training-only monthly mean adjustment gives approximately 0.675 and 0.579 and does not restore native-forcing performance. The useful result is sensitivity to the forcing–target relationship; a lower score against a native-forced target is not evidence that CHIRPS is a poorer rainfall observation.",
"The observed rainfall–storage test provides complementary evidence. A twelve-month accumulation is selected nationally with a one-month TWSA delay and two-month GWSA delay. Held-out correlations are 0.680 and 0.606, with 79 and 76 paired months and year-permutation p-values of 0.001 and 0.004. The association supports a hydroclimatic role in observed anomalies but does not uniquely identify a climate-only groundwater process."
]),
('3.3 The represented human perturbation does not explain the full residual',[
"The additive national TWSA OLS slopes are −8.36 mm/year observed, −0.54 naturalized, −0.49 modeled human, −7.81 residual and −7.32 discrepancy. Their exact point-trend identities distinguish the accounting partition from the non-additive Sen summaries. The two-year-block interval for the modeled-human term is approximately −0.96 to −0.005 mm/year, close to zero at its upper limit and sensitive to the conditional uncertainty specification.",
"For groundwater the corresponding slopes are −7.28, −0.21, +0.06, −7.07 and −7.13 mm/year. The paired modeled-human interval spans approximately −0.01 to +0.13 mm/year, while residual and discrepancy intervals remain negative. Thus the historical-human model does not reproduce the magnitude of the observation-based groundwater residual trend in the present experiment.",
"The national discrepancy-magnitude diagnostics are 0.938 for TWSA and 0.991 for GWSA, with principal conditional intervals approximately 0.822–0.997 and 0.978–0.999. These values express the relative size of modeled terms, not a percentage of human depletion. The residual and discrepancy series are highly similar, but that similarity cannot determine whether omitted management or other structural error dominates the unexplained difference."
]),
('3.4 Groundwater agreement changes with panel and time support',[
"The longest-overlap national residual–head correlations are 0.127 for TWSA and 0.037 for GWSA over 18 and 17 annual pairs. Coordinate-station bootstrap intervals are broad: approximately −0.277 to 0.601 and −0.304 to 0.466. Climate-controlled correlations are 0.102 and 0.001, and detrended correlations are −0.046 and −0.107. These results do not establish independent groundwater confirmation of the residual.",
"The wider-history fixed-eligibility panel yields raw correlations of 0.657 and 0.511, apparently stronger than the primary series. However, the all-well 2013–2022 comparison and strict balanced panel share only eight usable storage years because mission-gap availability rules remain in force. GWSA residual correlations are 0.029 and approximately −0.0004, respectively. The strict panel comprises 189 coordinate groups. Its near-zero relationship shows why an attractive wider-history panel correlation should not be read as validation without a time-matched check.",
"The well-level trend screen retains 382 annual wells and 225 coordinate-station trajectories. Their median head slope is approximately −0.016 m/year; 54.2 percent of station-median slopes are negative, with a station-resampling interval of 47.1–60.0 percent. This neither establishes a dominant national head direction in the monitored population nor supplies a mass-storage rate. The observation network is not a random sample of Thai aquifers.",
"Across basins, available annual pairs and station groups are uneven, and some comparisons lack adequate support. The 460 panel/component checks retain those insufficient cases. Missing evidence is not classified as successful or failed pumping validation. The combined results identify a monitoring and interpretation limit that remains visible even though the climate emulators reproduce their parent model well."
])]
DISCUSSION=[
('4.1 National direction and local attribution require different evidence',[
"The results support concern about broad storage decline, but they do not support a product-independent ranking of basin depletion. This distinction matters because policy applications often seek a local priority list from a single map. The present common-support comparison shows that processing-chain choices can preserve a national sign while substantially changing the ordering of smaller reporting units. Conditional trend significance alone cannot resolve that spatial uncertainty.",
"Native mapping helps explain why raster detail is not a sufficient answer. A basin may intersect many quarter-degree cells while sharing the same mapped native elements with neighbouring basins. Regularization can extend that dependence further. Our concentration and overlap diagnostics therefore qualify spatial interpretation without claiming to estimate the true averaging kernels or inversion covariance. They are useful screening measures, not substitutes for a full geodetic error analysis.",
"The groundwater product comparison is additionally sensitive to compartment structure. The CSR-plus-Noah and native G3P chains do not isolate a single processing difference, so their spread should not be labelled a gravity-only uncertainty. A further physically harmonized multi-product study could separate these effects more fully. The present result is already sufficient to show that exact local magnitudes and rankings require more support than a stable national direction."
]),
('4.2 Climate reconstruction is not counterfactual validation',[
"The memory ablation is a positive result: antecedent meteorology materially improves reconstruction of naturalized storage anomalies. Because the comparison holds the statistical family and chronology constant, the gain is more informative than a contest among many algorithms evaluated only on raw monthly seasonality. Removing seasonal harmonics still leaves substantial memory-model skill, supporting the role of antecedent weather within the parent system.",
"The forcing replacement limits how far that success can be interpreted. A frozen model loses anomaly skill when its precipitation input changes, even after a limited training-only mean adjustment. This is expected when a model approximates a target generated with particular forcing. It warns against presenting a high native-forcing emulator score as independent evidence that the parent model supplies the true unobserved climatic trajectory.",
"A second naturalized hydrological model and forcing ensemble would help assess structural counterfactual uncertainty, but that experiment has not been performed here. Actual-ET or remotely sensed soil products could improve empirical reconstruction while also carrying management information. They should therefore be evaluated according to their intended predictive role rather than automatically added to a model labelled purely climatic."
]),
('4.3 Residual disagreement and the groundwater validation problem',[
"The paired decomposition shows why the residual should retain an unresolved interpretation. Most of its trend lies in the discrepancy from the historical-human simulation, while the represented direct-human perturbation is small. It would be unjustified to call the full discrepancy measurement error, just as it would be unjustified to call the full residual human depletion. Both unrepresented management and model or observation mismatch remain plausible.",
"The groundwater comparison does not resolve that ambiguity. Heads are an independent observation type, but the retained network lacks the aquifer properties required for volumetric conversion and is uneven in time and space. The strict time-matched panel gives weak groundwater residual agreement, while the wider-history eligibility panel looks stronger. The latter contrast is itself useful evidence about validation design and should not be hidden by selecting the best-looking panel.",
"Climate control also has a precise mathematical limit. Since R=O−C, residualizing both O and R against the same C yields the same storage residual vector. Similar partial correlations for observed and residual storage are therefore expected from the accounting identity. They do not demonstrate that the true climatic head component has been removed. Independent pumping, screened-interval and recharge information would be needed for a stronger causal test.",
"The annual record count further limits apparent statistical power. Hundreds of wells do not create hundreds of independent temporal replicates of a national storage trajectory. The balanced comparison contains only eight usable storage years, and station-resampling intervals condition on those years. Treating the well count as the correlation sample size would greatly overstate the evidence."
]),
('4.4 Contribution, limitations and decision use',[
"The proposed contribution is an integrated, auditable credibility assessment rather than a new depletion algorithm. It connects documented gravity support, controlled climate-memory reconstruction, a discrepancy-preserving paired experiment and observation-network sensitivity across an entire principal-basin framework. The empirical combination reveals a mismatch between robust broad decline, unstable local ranks and weak groundwater residual confirmation. That mismatch is a scientific result, not a reason to force an attribution percentage.",
"Several limitations remain. Only one naturalized hydrological-model/forcing pairing supplies the principal counterfactual. Noah and G3P compartments differ in depth or filtering. Native mapping is not a complete gravity-error model. Network bootstrap intervals omit temporal uncertainty, and the annual head observations are sparse summaries with incomplete aquifer metadata. These conditions limit causal and volumetric interpretation even where conditional tests or parent-model scores are strong.",
"For decision support, the appropriate outputs are evidence-qualified storage screens and monitoring priorities. A basin with large decline but poor product agreement needs source and compartment checks. A basin with inadequate wells needs representative aquifer-specific monitoring. The interactive system retains these qualifications beside downloadable results, while the executable package allows reviewers to reproduce the calculations and inspect insufficient-support cases."
])]
CONCLUSION=[
"A documented CSR-based national record supports declining TWSA and derived GWSA over the analyzed periods, but common-support product comparisons reveal weak stability in basin rankings. Climate memory strongly improves naturalized-model reconstruction beyond current weather and seasonality, while precipitation-source replacement exposes forcing dependence. These are useful reconstruction findings, not independent validation of the counterfactual or of human depletion.",
"The paired experiment retains the exact residual identity and shows that observation–historical-model discrepancy is much larger than the represented direct-human trend. Groundwater-head agreement remains weak under longest-overlap and strict common-period balanced-panel comparisons. The results therefore do not validate treating the entire residual as human-caused groundwater loss.",
"The study's contribution is to make these inferential limits measurable and reproducible across Thailand's principal basins. A stronger attribution requires additional naturalized models, physically harmonized compartments and aquifer-specific head, storage-coefficient, pumping and reservoir records. The present evidence supports regional storage concern and targeted investigation while preserving uncertainty about local severity and cause."
]

